('alict_00894', 4, 'Computer Systems & Architecture', 'Which of the following statement(s) about the types of ROM is', '["Input", "Processing", "Output", "Storage", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "Input". This reflects how this hardware component/device is defined and used in a standard computer system.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Computer Systems & Architecture', NULL, 894, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_00895', 4, 'Computer Systems & Architecture', 'How is data stored in ROM?', '["Temporarily using electrical charges", "Permanently using non-volatile methods", "Dynamically using transistors", "Temporarily using magnetic fields", "Permanently using volatile methods"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ROM (Read-Only Memory) stores data permanently using non-volatile methods. ROM u.ska Data in ROM is written during the manufacturing process or through specific programming (like in EEPROM)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Computer Systems & Architecture', NULL, 895, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_00896', 4, 'Computer Systems & Architecture', 'What is the main advantage of EEPROM over EPROM?', '["It can be erased using ultraviolet light", "It allows for data erasure and reprogramming through electrical signals. th", "It has a much higher storage capacity", "It is faster than RAM", "It can only be written once"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: This is a characteristic of not EEPROM. EPROM requires UV light exposure to erase data. EPROM Option 2: Correct. This is the key advantage of EEPROM. Data can be erased and rewritten making it more convenient and easier to update than EPROM', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Computer Systems & Architecture', NULL, 896, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
